Give the echo canceller room for an echo that comes earlier

When the sound server misses its deadlines (PipeWire on a busy computer) it can leave out
some of what an application played or recorded around the missed cycles, and the echo then
reaches the microphone one or more cycles earlier, in samples, than the echo canceller's
alignment: about 10 ms a cycle at a quantum of 480 or 512, 21 ms at 1024. AEC3 places the
echo 2 ms (delay_headroom_samples 32 at 16 kHz) after the start of its linear filter, so an
echo even a few ms earlier was out of the filter's reach, and the other person's voice went
back to them for a whole turn until the delay estimator found the echo again, a second or
more later. An echo that comes later (silence played on an underflow) stays inside the
filter.

The processing now asks AEC3 for 512 samples (8 blocks, 32 ms) of headroom, in its mono and
multichannel configurations. The filters keep WebRTC's lengths (13 blocks, 12 in the initial
state), which with the echo 8 blocks in reach 5 blocks past it (4 in the initial state).
After a reset AEC3 keeps only 2 blocks of headroom until it finds the delay again; keeping
the configured headroom there too made a call's start worse (WebRTC patch 0010's header), so
it is left as it is.

As first written, this patch also made the refined and coarse filters 8 blocks longer (21),
so that they reached as far after the echo as before. Those longer filters converge more
slowly, and a call's start with speakers sent the other side's voice back for seconds: with
a synthesised voice reading text that does not repeat (mic.sh unmute, UNMUTE_FAR_FILE), in
one session, 10 runs each, a call's start sent it back for 2.7 s at the median (0 to 7.6) at
-49 dBFS at the loudest, against 0 s (0 to 2.0) and -66 dBFS with WebRTC's own configuration
(p 0.008 for both) and 0.5 s at -51 dBFS with the library before WebRTC patch 0010; in
another session, 16 ms of headroom with the filters 8 blocks longer sent it back for 6.8 s
at the median at -46 dBFS (p 0.005 and 0.019 against the library before patch 0010), and
the initial-state filters lengthened as well, for 5.8 s at -35 dBFS (6 runs). As it is now
(32 ms, WebRTC's filters), in that session: 0 s at the median (0 to 1.4) at -54 dBFS,
against 0 s (0 to 7.7) at -56 dBFS before patch 0010 (p 1.0 and 0.34) and 0 s at -60 dBFS
with WebRTC's own configuration. Its unmutes with speakers came back 0.3 s at the median
(1.6 s before patch 0010, which is what shortens them, not this patch).

Measured in the call lab with the filters 8 blocks longer (tools/call-lab/mic.sh echo,
ECHO_STALL stopping the session's PipeWire now and then, the echo's move timed in the
samples each copy wrote and read), without the patch, then with 16, 32 and 48 ms of
headroom:
- moves up to 16 ms earlier followed by a turn let through: 30 in 68, 2 in 66, 4 in 73,
  9 in 63; moves of 16 to 32 ms: 26 in 79, 15 in 78, 4 in 71, 11 in 66; over 32 ms: 13 in
  53, 9 in 53, 8 in 52, 10 in 51;
- PipeWire's quantum left free (256 samples there, as on a desktop), stalls of 20 and 40 ms
  (four runs each with the measuring layer): 25 turns let through in 131, 5 in 132, 5 in
  132, 14 in 129;
- the quantum fixed at 480, stalls of 40 and 80 ms: 19 in 128, 14 in 130, 7 in 131, 13 in
  130;
- without stalls: none in 363, 2 in 193, none in 189, 4 in 98; a call's first turn 76 dB
  under one's own voice at the median without the patch and with it, later turns 73 and
  72 dB; WebRTC's audio threads used 2.88 % of a core with it and without.

Measured again in one session with WebRTC patches 0010 to 0012, interleaved with the
library as published (without this patch) and with every echo controller restarted: the
echo check let 10 turns through in 136 with the quantum free and stalls of 20 and 40 ms (35
in 137 without this patch), 4 in 110 at 480 with stalls of 40 and 80 ms (28 in 109).

As it is now, in one session with WebRTC patches 0010 to 0012, 6 runs each, interleaved
with WebRTC's own configuration, with the filters 8 blocks longer and with 16 ms of
headroom and the filters 8 blocks longer: with the quantum free and stalls of 20 and 40 ms
the echo check let 4 turns through in 159, against 51 in 111 with WebRTC's configuration,
7 in 154 with the longer filters and 8 in 151 with 16 ms; at 480 with stalls of 40 and
80 ms, 11 in 148, against 39 in 121, 12 in 151 and 21 in 141. After each of one's own
words in a call with speakers and a steady tone on the other side (mic.sh unmute:speakers,
6 runs each, 18 unmutes), the tone came back in the next 0.25 s at -55 dBFS at the median
of each unmute's worst, -36 at the worst, against -58 and -34 with WebRTC's configuration
and -47 and -30 with the longer filters (p 0.28 and 0.17): AEC3's own, which holds its
gentler suppression for 200 ms after one's own voice (dominant_nearend_detection,
hold_duration 50 blocks).

With the filters 8 blocks longer, a known cost was a far side that repeats itself exactly
(not measured again with WebRTC's filters). The unmute check's own far side
(mic.sh unmute, UNMUTE_FAR=words) is the same 2.1 s phrase every 3 s, three words made of
a sawtooth whose pitch falls within each syllable, between 160 and 106 Hz: with it, a call's start with speakers sent
the other side's voice back for 4.8 s at the median (0 to 8.9) at -40 dBFS at the loudest,
where AEC3 with WebRTC's headroom took 0.9 s (0 to 3.7) at -52 dBFS (8 runs each, p 0.05;
restarted rather than made anew, 4.9 s, the same). The lab's logs show the same delay found
(7 blocks) in the same time and the filters converged as soon; why the signal that repeats
comes back longer is not known. With speech-like noise that repeats only every 30 s
(tone.py speech, as the echo check's voices) there is no difference (0 s heard, p
0.96 and 0.47). The synthesised voice's call start, which an earlier session of 8 runs
each had within the runs' scatter (p 0.06 and 0.22), was the longer filters' cost
measured above.

diff --git a/src/rtc_audio_processing_impl.cc b/src/rtc_audio_processing_impl.cc
index 39cf151..3f8d4d6 100644
--- a/src/rtc_audio_processing_impl.cc
+++ b/src/rtc_audio_processing_impl.cc
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
 #include "rtc_audio_processing_impl.h"
 
 #include "api/audio/builtin_audio_processing_builder.h"
+#include "api/audio/echo_canceller3_config.h"
 #include "api/environment/environment_factory.h"
 #include "modules/audio_processing/audio_buffer.h"
 #include "modules/audio_processing/ns/ns_common.h"
@@ -9,6 +10,27 @@
 
 namespace libwebrtc {
 
+namespace {
+
+// Fogata: room for an echo that comes earlier than the one the echo canceller has
+// found. When the sound server misses its deadlines (PipeWire, a busy computer) it can
+// leave out some of what was played or recorded, and the echo then reaches the
+// microphone one or more of its cycles (10 to 21 ms each) earlier than before. With
+// WebRTC's default headroom (32 samples at 16 kHz: 2 ms) the linear filter cannot reach
+// such an echo, and a whole turn of the other's voice went back to them until the delay
+// estimator found the echo again, a second or more later. 512 samples (8 blocks,
+// 32 ms) of headroom. The filters keep WebRTC's lengths: 8 blocks longer, they took so
+// much longer to converge at a call's start that the other side's voice came back for
+// seconds.
+constexpr size_t kDelayHeadroomSamples = 512;
+
+webrtc::EchoCanceller3Config WithEarlierEchoRoom(webrtc::EchoCanceller3Config config) {
+  config.delay.delay_headroom_samples = kDelayHeadroomSamples;
+  return config;
+}
+
+}  // namespace
+
 class CustomProcessingAdapter : public webrtc::CustomProcessing {
  public:
   CustomProcessingAdapter() = default;
@@ -84,6 +106,10 @@ RTCAudioProcessingImpl::RTCAudioProcessingImpl(const webrtc::Environment& env) {
       render_pre_processor_);
 
   apm_ = webrtc::BuiltinAudioProcessingBuilder()
+             .SetEchoCancellerConfig(
+                 WithEarlierEchoRoom(webrtc::EchoCanceller3Config()),
+                 WithEarlierEchoRoom(
+                     webrtc::EchoCanceller3Config::CreateDefaultMultichannelConfig()))
              .SetCapturePostProcessing(std::move(capture_post_processor))
              .SetRenderPreProcessing(std::move(render_pre_processor))
              .Build(env);
